/**
 * @file spl_examples.dox
 * @brief Canonical examples and common authoring mistakes for the repository DSL.
 *
 * @page spl_examples_guide SPL examples and common mistakes
 * @tableofcontents
 *
 * @section spl_examples_intro Purpose
 *
 * This page complements @ref spl_authoring_guide by showing the canonical SPL
 * fixtures kept in the repository and by collecting parser and semantic pitfalls
 * that are easy to miss when writing files by hand.
 *
 * @section spl_examples_nav_sec Navigation and related pages
 *
 * Recommended reading order:
 *
 * - @ref documentation_guide
 * - @ref spl_authoring_guide
 * - @ref spl_examples_guide
 * - @ref spl_parser
 *
 * Related pages:
 *
 * - @ref feature_analysis

 * @section spl_examples_canonical Canonical valid examples
 *
 * The following fixtures are the canonical positive examples used by the
 * repository:
 *
 * - `spl_specs/new_dsl/valid/unconstrained_2f.spl`
 * - `spl_specs/new_dsl/valid/fx_implies_fy.spl`
 * - `spl_specs/new_dsl/valid/fx_implies_fy_missing_s0.spl`
 * - `spl_specs/new_dsl/valid/factorial_paper.spl`
 *
 * Documentation-facing copies live under
 * `external_documentation/examples_new_dsl/`.
 *
 * @subsection spl_examples_unconstrained Unconstrained two-feature example
 *
 * @code{.text}
 * SPL TwoFeatureUnconstrained
 * FEATURES 2 fx fy
 * @endcode
 *
 * Use this when you want the full candidate system space `P(F)` with no
 * additional constraints.
 *
 * @subsection spl_examples_implication Implication example
 *
 * @code{.text}
 * SPL TwoFeatureFxImpliesFy
 *
 * FEATURES 2 fx fy
 *
 * CONSTRAINTS
 *   ASSERT fx -> fy
 * END
 * @endcode
 *
 * This is the smallest canonical example with a non-trivial valid system space.
 *
 * @subsection spl_examples_missing Missing-system example
 *
 * @code{.text}
 * SPL TwoFeatureFxImpliesFyMissingS0
 *
 * FEATURES 2 fx fy
 *
 * CONSTRAINTS
 *   ASSERT fx -> fy
 * END
 *
 * MISSING_SYSTEMS 1 END
 * @endcode
 *
 * This is the paper-facing missing-system scenario used in the repository.
 *
 * @subsection spl_examples_factorial Factorial paper example
 *
 * @code{.text}
 * SPL FactorialPaper
 *
 * FEATURES 4 r i l c
 *
 * CONSTRAINTS
 *   ASSERT COUNT(r, i) = 1
 * END
 * @endcode
 *
 * This demonstrates the repository `COUNT(...)` syntax for a cardinality-style
 * constraint.
 *
 * @section spl_examples_invalid Useful invalid patterns
 *
 * The repository also keeps negative fixtures under `spl_specs/new_dsl/invalid/`
 * for parser and semantic rejection tests. Typical invalid patterns include:
 *
 * - `FEATURES` count does not match the number of names that follow,
 * - a formula references an undeclared feature name,
 * - a `COUNT(...)` list repeats the same feature name,
 * - `COUNT(...) IN [a,b]` is written with `a > b`,
 * - an available or missing system ID is not valid,
 * - the same system ID appears in both availability sections,
 * - both availability sections are present but do not partition the valid system
 *   space.
 *
 * @section spl_examples_pitfalls Common authoring pitfalls
 *
 * @subsection spl_examples_pitfall_order Referencing names before FEATURES
 *
 * `FEATURES` must appear before formulas that reference feature names. Declare
 * the basis first, then add `CONSTRAINTS`.
 *
 * @subsection spl_examples_pitfall_repeat Repeating top-level sections
 *
 * The intended grammar uses each top-level section at most once. The current
 * parser does not reject repeated sections explicitly; later parsed payload may
 * overwrite earlier payload. Treat repeated sections as invalid authoring style
 * even if a particular file appears to parse.
 *
 * @subsection spl_examples_pitfall_end Omitting END
 *
 * `CONSTRAINTS` must use `END`.
 *
 * `AVAILABLE_SYSTEMS`, `MISSING_SYSTEMS`, and `CATEGORY_FILTER` can terminate at
 * the next top-level section or end-of-file, but explicitly writing `END` is
 * the clearest and most stable style.
 *
 * @subsection spl_examples_pitfall_filter CATEGORY_FILTER is not semantic input
 *
 * `CATEGORY_FILTER` changes reporting only. It does not change parsing, system
 * validity, derived-feature generation, or feature simplification.
 *
 * @subsection spl_examples_pitfall_count COUNT(...) is repository syntax
 *
 * `COUNT(...)` is supported by the repository, but it is not the paper's own
 * surface notation. In theory-facing explanations, treat it as a repository
 * encoding of the paper's arithmetic cardinality idea.
 *
 * @section spl_examples_next Where to go next
 *
 * For the full authoring walkthrough, see @ref spl_authoring_guide.
 *
 * For the parser data structures and executable semantics, see:
 *
 * - @ref spl_parser
 * - @ref feature_isolation::theory_formula_t "theory_formula_t"
 * - @ref feature_isolation::spl_specification_t "spl_specification_t"
 * - @ref feature_isolation::spl_processor_t "spl_processor_t"
 */